+**Pros/Cons in design documents:**
+- Architecture decisions get a "Pros / Cons / Why we chose this" block.
+- Format: simple lists, no elaborate tables.
+- Proportional to decision weight — a minor choice gets one line each, a major choice gets a paragraph.
+
+Example:
+> **Pros:** Reproducible, version-pinned, clean patch mechanism
+> **Cons:** Must maintain the patch when upstream updates
+> **Why we chose this:** The alternative (post-activation scripts) is fragile and not reproducible
+
+**Vertical-slice discipline:**
+Every task in the design must be a thin vertical slice — independently demoable, touching all layers needed for that slice of functionality. Reject horizontal tasks like "set up all database tables", "build all API endpoints", "wire all UI components" that cut across features.
+
+**Reject:** T1 Create schema for users/posts/comments; T2 Build CRUD endpoints for all entities; T3 Wire up UI for everything.
+**Require:** T1 User registration (schema + endpoint + UI); T2 Create post (depends on T1); T3 Comments (depends on T2).
+**Escape hatch for cross-cutting prerequisites:** A task that is a shared prerequisite (add a library, set up a build tool, create shared types) can be horizontal, but must carry `**Task type:** prerequisite` in the task block and sit at the root of the dependency graph (`depends on: none`). The vertical-slice rule applies to feature work, not scaffolding.

