+## Strategy Selection Heuristics
+
+Apply these rules in order. Take the first match. Record the match and reason in the spec's `## Execution Strategy` section. There are only two strategies — no sequential mode.
+
+1. **Agent teams** when ALL of: ≥4 independent tasks; cross-cutting coordination required (shared integration points, negotiated contracts, coordinated schema/API changes across streams); reviews need to look at work holistically across streams, not just per-task; the feature is large enough that a persistent team with messaging pays off.
+2. **Subagents** — the default in every other case. Use when: 1-3 tasks, OR ≥4 tasks worked independently with clear file ownership and no inter-task communication, OR any work where per-task review is sufficient. A single-task job still uses Subagents — you just dispatch one subagent and run the per-task review.
+
+**Tiebreakers:** If torn between Subagents and teams, choose Subagents. Multi-language designs are NOT a reason to escalate to teams — Subagents with different agent types handles it.
+
+**Do NOT ask the user to confirm the selection.** Announce it and write it into the spec. The user can countermand it explicitly; otherwise proceed.
+
+## Coding Rules Section (Mandatory)
+
+Every spec MUST contain a `## Coding Rules` section near the end, alongside `## Execution Strategy`. The section pairs each task with the rule files the implementer must read before touching code, plus a Polish row naming the rules the polish subagent loads.
+
+```markdown
+## Coding Rules
+- Task 1 [<task title>]: <rule names or `none`>
+- Task 2 [<task title>]: <rule names or `none`>
+- ...
+- Polish: <rule names or `none`>
+```
+
+Rule names are path-relative tokens (no extension, forward slashes) under `~/.config/claude/rules/` — for example `languages/rust`, `base/git`, `frameworks/react`. The literal token `none` (case-insensitive, bare, no parentheses) is allowed wherever a rule-name list is expected. The Polish row is mandatory. writing-plans propagates the section verbatim into the plan header and adds a dedicated `**Polish:** post-implementation-polish → <rules>` line that polish-phase dispatch reads. If you are not sure which rules apply to a task, write `none` and let the implementer escalate if a rule is needed.
+
+## Task Dependency Graph Section (Mandatory)
+
+Every spec MUST contain a `## Task Dependency Graph` section alongside `## Coding Rules` and `## Execution Strategy`. Assign each task a `depends on:` field.
+
+| Task | Depends On |
+|------|------------|
+| T1   | none       |
+| T2   | T1         |
+| T3   | T1         |
+| T4   | T2, T3     |
+
+The table is a summary; per-task `depends on:` fields are authoritative if they drift. Tasks in the spec MUST appear in topological order. Validation: no cycles, no references to undefined task IDs, no duplicate task IDs.
+
+
 ## Visual Companion

 A browser-based companion for showing mockups, diagrams, and visual options during brainstorming. Available as a tool — not a mode. Accepting the companion means it's available for questions that benefit from visual treatment; it does NOT mean every question goes through the browser.
